id: records/2026-09-05-voices-answers-and-public-feedback-clarification
title: "Voices as attributed perspectives; answers as the bridge from lacunae to moves"
kind: clarification-record
status: proposed-canonical-backfill
recorded: 2026-09-05
supersedes_history: false
mutationAuthorized: false
externalEffectsAuthorized: false

source_observation: >
  Dave recognized that an earlier Yawn.ai voice system already contains a seed
  of the current architecture: multiple internal or external standpoints respond
  to a situation, questions reveal lacunae, candidate answers compete or
  cooperate, and the resulting model informs moves. He connected this to public
  comments on media: a creator publishes a projection, other intelligences
  respond through their own apertures, and those responses can help refine the
  creator's model, expression, experiment, or future behavior.

preserve:
  - >
    Questions expose lacunae. Attributed answer candidates reduce, reframe, or
    deepen those lacunae. The resulting answer field informs the next warranted
    question, hold, test, decision, or move.
  - >
    Different Yawnbots, humans, models, roles, historical selves, and public
    commenters may offer distinct perspectives on the same question.
  - >
    Public response can become a visible input to recursive improvement when the
    source projection, respondent aperture, evidence relation, consequence, and
    resulting update remain inspectable.
  - >
    The system should show why one question, answer, or move currently leads and
    what evidence would change that position.
  - >
    The earlier voice, public-answer, expression-synthesis, and feedback-
    reintegration code should be reconciled rather than discarded or duplicated.

correct:
  - >
    A voice is not automatically a Yawnbot. It is an attributed expression from
    a standpoint. An already-existing Yawnbot may produce a voice; a generated
    voice earns persistent bot status only if it independently satisfies the
    Agent/Yawn persistence threshold.
  - >
    A question does not necessarily create one strict hierarchy of answers.
    Answers may oppose, support, qualify, reframe, apply under different
    conditions, address different scopes, or remain unknown. The underlying
    model is relational; the interface may show a ranked answer frontier.
  - >
    Comments are not direct rewards. They are heterogeneous feedback events:
    experience reports, factual claims, questions, preferences, value
    disagreements, requests, resonance, counterexamples, consequences,
    manipulation, spam, or unknown input.
  - >
    Positive comments do not prove the creator was right. Negative comments do
    not prove failure. Popularity, conviction, salience, evidence, affectedness,
    and decision relevance must remain separate.
  - >
    Unless a formal reward and policy-update contract exists, call the mechanism
    attributed feedback reintegration or perspective-conditioned model revision,
    not reinforcement learning.
  - >
    Recursive improvement should optimize a named, principal-ratified outcome
    under safety, evidence, affected-party, and authority constraints—not simply
    fewer objections, more praise, or longer engagement.

unified_shape:
  sequence:
    - question_or_lacuna
    - permitted_perspectives
    - attributed_answer_candidates
    - answer_relationship_and_evidence_graph
    - ranked_answer_frontier
    - synthesis_with_dissent_and_unknowns
    - next_warranted_turn
    - principal_choice_or_hold
    - authorized_move_or_publication
    - consequence_and_response
    - attributed_feedback_events
    - proof_and_calibration
    - updated_questions_answers_moves_and_apertures
  note: >
    This sequence belongs inside the established Crystallization, Learning, and
    Execution loops. It does not create a fourth canonical loop.

voice_definition:
  statement: >
    A voice is what an attributed standpoint would say here, through this
    aperture, about this question, claim, relationship, expression, or move.
  possible_origins:
    - current_self_report
    - historical_self
    - named_human_participant
    - invited_collaborator
    - local_yawnbot
    - connected_agent
    - advocate_or_challenger_role
    - public_commenter
    - research_or_measurement
    - model_simulation
    - unknown_missing_perspective
  boundaries:
    - "voice != Agent"
    - "voice != Yawnbot"
    - "voice != stable belief"
    - "voice != psychological diagnosis"
    - "voice != authority"
    - "voice != proof"

answer_frontier:
  statement: >
    The answer frontier is the small current set of candidate answers whose
    evaluation would most improve orientation or movement. It is a View over a
    larger answer/evidence graph.
  show:
    - leading_answer_or_synthesis_if_warranted
    - meaningful_alternatives
    - material_dissent
    - applicability_conditions
    - source_and_perspective
    - evidence_for_and_against
    - confidence_or_unknown
    - conviction_or_preference_if_expressed
    - what_would_change_the_position
  do_not_conflate:
    - question_priority
    - answer_credence
    - perspective_relevance
    - social_popularity
    - user_conviction
    - emotional_salience
    - move_leverage

external_comment_example:
  publication: >
    Dave publishes one dated, provisioned expression from a Yawn through a
    Public Story or other public aperture.
  response: >
    A commenter responds using only the public projection and their own
    experience, values, incentives, and assumptions.
  system_behavior: >
    Preserve the comment and its context; classify its possible relation as a
    proposal; connect it to the relevant question, claim, or move; estimate its
    evidence and decision relevance; surface consequential minority feedback;
    and ask whether it should change the model, expression, audience, test,
    future move, or nothing.
  principal_boundary: >
    Dave may accept, reject, reframe, test, or hold the feedback. The commenter
    cannot silently rewrite Dave's values or private Yawn, and Dave cannot treat
    the comment as access to the commenter's full model.

self_improvement_boundary:
  valid_learning_chain: >
    publish or move -> response or consequence -> attributed feedback ->
    classification proposal -> evidence evaluation -> model/rank delta -> next
    test -> later consequence -> calibration.
  invalid_shortcuts:
    - "like -> truth"
    - "agreement -> proof"
    - "criticism -> failure"
    - "engagement -> value"
    - "comment volume -> representativeness"
    - "model consensus -> independent evidence"
    - "user reaction -> policy authority"

existing_code_reconciliation:
  surface_voice_generator: >
    Preserve its insight that needs, tensions, constraints, practical
    job-scoping, and deeper probes can be surfaced before movement. Generalize
    forced position/counter pairs into a multi-answer perspective field and
    retain labels such as bias, need, emotion, or IFS part as optional attributed
    hypotheses requiring correction, not diagnoses.
  voice_cards: >
    Preserve easy reaction and comment controls, but record whether the response
    means recognition, conviction, preference, relevance, or something else.
    Do not let a slider automatically increase truth or self-model confidence.
  control_level_detector: >
    Preserve Regulation/Task/Goal/Identity/Meaning as a possible presentation or
    routing lens. The keyword detector is a low-confidence proposal, not a fixed
    hierarchy of cognition, diagnostic type, or permission to suppress mixed
    levels.
  feedback_reintegration: >
    Preserve source feedback and its possible link to lacunae, moves, or
    variants. Replace brittle canonical keyword classification and immediate
    writes with attributed proposals, explicit evidence relations, and governed
    update targets.
  answer_agent: >
    Preserve the inverse Question/Answer intuition. Remove assumptions that every
    answer adds positive coherence. An answer can increase, reduce, split,
    dispute, reframe, or leave coherence unchanged. Processing failure creates
    no positive epistemic credit.
  expression_synthesis: >
    Preserve Expression(t)=f(Tree(t)) as a View: answers can sharpen what the
    Yawn currently expresses. Do not allow answer count, tree density, or
    internal consistency to substitute for evidence or erase dissent, source
    scope, uncertainty, and alternatives.

implementation_priority:
  first: >
    Audit and model the existing voice, answer, comment, expression, and feedback
    data flows before creating another agent family.
  second: >
    Introduce richer perspective and answer-candidate provenance through
    compatibility adapters.
  third: >
    Bind the perspective field into Questions First, ranked deliberation,
    public/shared apertures, and historical replay.
  fourth: >
    Test one real publication-response-revision cycle against a plain comment
    inbox baseline.

proof_questions:
  - "Does the perspective field find better questions or moves than a single-answer model?"
  - "Can users distinguish a generated voice from their own belief and another person's actual position?"
  - "Does the system preserve high-value minority evidence instead of following popularity?"
  - "Do external comments lead to traceable improvements in communication, model accuracy, or outcomes rather than approval-seeking?"
  - "Can replay show exactly which answer or feedback event changed a rank?"
  - "Does the added perspective metadata reduce repeated errors enough to justify its complexity?"

